WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.441
A team lost two positions last year, and

2
00:00:02.441 --> 00:00:03.810
the work stayed the same.

3
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.360
Two people are on leave.

4
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:08.555
Then a difficult new case comes in, the

5
00:00:08.555 --> 00:00:11.010
kind one case manager always handles well.

6
00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.704
And that case manager is already covering more

7
00:00:13.704 --> 00:00:14.240
than anyone.

8
00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:15.690
So it goes to them.

9
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:16.400
Again.

10
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:17.950
It's efficient, though.

11
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.000
Today.

12
00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:22.321
The burnout research points to six areas of

13
00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:25.251
work life that matter: workload, control, reward, community,

14
00:00:25.251 --> 00:00:26.350
fairness and values.

15
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.930
Giving the hard case to the same person

16
00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.040
every time touches at least three of them.

17
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.918
So the supervisor raises it in a one-on-one,

18
00:00:33.918 --> 00:00:36.246
and the case manager says, I'm fine, it's

19
00:00:36.246 --> 00:00:37.410
just a busy season.

20
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.120
But the supervisor has seen late-night emails for

21
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.160
most of the month.

22
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.102
The mistake would be to argue about whether

23
00:00:43.102 --> 00:00:43.510
they're fine.

24
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:45.780
Because they'll just dig in.

25
00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:46.630
Right.

26
00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.800
Name what you've noticed, without judgment, and make

27
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:49.740
it about the work.

28
00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.648
I've seen a lot of late emails this

29
00:00:51.648 --> 00:00:51.850
month.

30
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.820
That tells me the load isn't working.

31
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:55.957
What's one thing I could take off or

32
00:00:55.957 --> 00:00:56.170
move?

33
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.393
So the supervisor owns the problem, instead of

34
00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:01.680
asking the person to admit they're struggling.

35
00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.030
Exactly.

36
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.420
Workload is a design problem.

37
00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.130
The supervisor has the levers.

38
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.300
Here's where I get nervous.

39
00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.939
A supervisor proposes a shared weekly view of

40
00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:13.330
caseloads.

41
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.777
As a newer person, I'd worry it'll be

42
00:01:15.777 --> 00:01:17.440
used to judge whoever is slower.

43
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.170
That worry is the whole design question.

44
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.290
Say clearly what the view is for: spotting

45
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.940
when one person is overloaded and moving work.

46
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:27.030
Not ranking anyone.

47
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.311
Agree it as a team, and review it

48
00:01:29.311 --> 00:01:30.020
after a month.

49
00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.110
And notice who doesn't speak.

50
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.957
If someone with years on the team stays

51
00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:36.860
silent, that's worth a private follow-up.

52
00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.090
Last piece.

53
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.787
The program manager asks the supervisor how the

54
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:41.600
team is doing.

55
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.486
Caseloads up thirty percent, two people on leave,

56
00:01:44.486 --> 00:01:45.430
two reports late.

57
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.620
The instinct is to say, we're managing.

58
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.110
And then nothing changes above you.

59
00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.636
Give the facts plainly, say what's at risk,

60
00:01:53.636 --> 00:01:55.853
and bring a choice: here's what we can

61
00:01:55.853 --> 00:01:58.069
keep on time, here's what will slip, which

62
00:01:58.069 --> 00:01:58.900
would you protect?

63
00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:00.510
That's not complaining.

64
00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.920
That's giving your manager something to decide.

65
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.610
And it protects your team.

66
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.704
That's what this course practices: seeing the drivers,

67
00:02:07.704 --> 00:02:10.469
holding the conversation, and changing how the work

68
00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:11.160
is shared.
